The two-thread drawing's submit thread (2912: draw loop, Execute, Present) runs above normal priority and opts out of EcoQoS (HOST_PRIO=0 leaves both at the Windows default).

On a hybrid CPU under Windows 10 (no Thread Director) every CPU part of a frame ran about 3x slower than on a desktop CPU of the same
single-thread speed (test10: guest 48.6 vs 13.2 ms, FIFO drain 42 vs 14, present 19 vs 10). The title's high-priority PPU threads run
above normal (sys_ppu_thread.c); the submit thread ran at normal priority and could be throttled, so it could lose its core to them or
be left on an efficiency core. It now runs one step above normal, like the port's FIFO drain thread, and asks Windows not to
throttle its execution speed (SetThreadInformation ThreadPowerThrottling, looked up at run time). One line at start:
"[PIPE] thread: priority above normal ok, EcoQoS off ok". Host scheduling only: nothing the title reads changes.

